the scale of child sexual abuse in  rotherham is devastating almost one and  a half thousand children many groomed by  gangs of mostly asian men and now a  report by the police watchdog has called  out the failings of the force that  should have protected them  they were treated like criminals and in  some cases some of those  poor young girls now have  criminal records  you know so in essence they were not  believed and told they were responsible  for their own behaviour when really they  were victims of really serious crime  the independent office for police  conduct looked at how south yorkshire's  force investigated child sex abuse  between 1997 and 2013.  they found there was a culture where  vulnerable individuals in the town were  seen as problems with police ignoring  glaring warning signs like finding  children in cars or even a bedroom with  older men  the iopc investigated 47 police officers  eight were found to have a case to  answer for misconduct six for gross  misconduct the most severe sanction was  a final written warning but no one was  sacked and in many cases officers had  retired and couldn't face disciplinary  proceedings  south yorkshire police have apologized  and say attitudes and procedures today  are very different from what they were  back then the challenge was that they  didn't recognize the vulnerability that  the these children were displaying and  didn't treat them  didn't treat them as children and didn't  treat them as victims why would they not  treat children as children  well i think that the main problem was  that people just didn't recognize the  vulnerability that was being displayed  but sammy woodhouse a survivor of  grooming is infuriated by this response  no one has been fired people have  retired they've got their pensions so it  kind of just feels to me that you know  they've been rewarded  um  and you know we're left picking all the  pieces up we've had our lives ruined  we're talking about children as young as  you know 10 11 13 14 years old and what  their excuses are well we didn't see  them as children how can you not see  those children  the report says policing has changed  considerably but in south yorkshire it's  also about building trust and making  sure those who are abused feel they'll  finally be listened to becky kotchel sky  news rotherham
 

